content.text:
Yes. The same preprocessing can be generalized across the other yearly papers, but it should be turned into a reusable script rather than replaying fixed coordinates.

The robust parts are already applicable:
- render each source page at high resolution;
- detect black question-number labels with OCR and nearby-pixel checks;
- place a blue anchor just above-left of each confirmed label;
- detect the footer region and crop at a separator line;
- crop outer margins while protecting blue-dot bounds;
- remove cover pages, Section A instruction headers, and trailing formula sheets.

The 2012 coordinates and crop values are specific to that scan, so they cannot be copied unchanged to 2013, 2018, etc. Different years can have changed layouts, page rotations, skew, footer positions, and question counts. A reusable pipeline should detect these dynamically and produce a verification report for uncertain anchors, especially when OCR confidence is low.

For each new year, the script can automatically create `preprocessed/anchored_<year>p1a.pdf`, then validate that every expected question number has one anchor, no anchor is cropped, and no footer/formula-sheet page remains. The exceptional cases such as Q14, Q25, and Q33 would be flagged for targeted review instead of silently being misplaced.

